 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The metering data schema contract determines the report service's data access approach. True gate: if the schema is unstable or unavailable the epic's approach is rewritten (gate action `rewrite`, recorded via `gated_by` on E002). |\n46\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The admin view's code is built against the report API and data shapes the report service produces. |\n47\t| E003 → E004 | Step 2 passive-component exception | Distributed Workloads is a passive component here (no code changes; the Dashboard reads Kueue quota CRs directly per its architecture context). Because a different team validates it, a separate validation Implementation epic owned by that team is generated; it validates the quota semantics of the built admin view. |\n48\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n49\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n50\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n51\t\n52\t## Component Mapping\n53\t\n54\t- **Dashboard** — active. RHOAI Dashboard team (per\n55\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`): the\n56\t  investigation, the report service, and the administrator view.\n57\t- **Distributed Workloads** — passive: no code changes required; the Dashboard\n58\t  reads Kueue quota (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue) through the Kubernetes API per\n59\t  its architecture context. Because the owning team (Distributed Workloads)\n60\t  is a different team than the one building the view, the validation becomes\n61\t  a separate validation Implementation epic (E004) rather than an acceptance\n62\t  criterion on the Dashboard epic.\n63\t- **Documentation** — docs-authoring epic covering HLR-4.\n64\t\n65\tComponent names are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n66\t\n67\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n68\t\n69\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n70\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n71\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002, E003 | Computation and serving of per-project GPU-hour reports (E002); administrator viewing over selectable time ranges (E003). |\n72\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E002 | **bundled** — CSV export and the stable API for finance tooling are presentation modes of the same report API surface that HLR-1's serving work builds; the epic is not separately schedulable by priority because splitting would build the same endpoint twice. |\n73\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 | **bundled** into E003 — the current-month consumption-vs-quota comparison renders in the same administrator view surface as HLR-1's reports; splitting would build the same UI surface twice. E004 covers the Kueue quota semantics validation half, owned by the Distributed Workloads team. |\n74\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | docs-authoring epic. Priority P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1); it does not push priority upstream. |\n75\t\n76\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n77\t\n78\t## Health Warnings\n79\t\n80\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy contains no\n81\t  Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance\n82\t  Criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Technical Approach section.\n83\t  Team assignments were taken from the architecture context files; epic\n84\t  acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n85\t- **External API surface placement** (non-blocking scope consideration):\n86\t  HLR-2's \"stable API for finance tooling\" commits an external API surface on\n87\t  the Dashboard, whose documented interfaces are Kubernetes-API-only with no\n88\t  private component APIs (dashboard.md). Placement and the stability\n89\t  commitment (versioning/deprecation expectations) of this API should be\n90\t  confirmed with the strategy author.\n91\t- **External timing risk**: the metering data schema finalizes only when\n92\t  RHAISTRAT-9990 lands — handled via tiered delivery (below) and the E001\n93\t  investigation gate.\n94\t- Epic sizes: all epics are sized within the ~2-week epic guideline; no\n95\t  overages are carried.\n96\t\n97\t## Tiered Delivery\n98\t\n99\tThe external dependency — the metering data schema owned by in-flight\n100\tRHAISTRAT-9990 — has uncertain timing (the strategy's single open question).\n101\t\n102\t- **Tier 1 (independent, starts immediately):** E001 — the risk-reduction\n103\t  investigation. It pins the consumption contract and stability verdict\n104\t  without waiting for RHAISTRAT-9990 to land.\n105\t- **Tier 2 (gated on metering data being consumable):** E002, E003, E004,\n106\t  E005 — all consume metering data (directly or via the report service) and\n107\t  proceed once the consumption contract is agreed.\n108\t\n109\tNo conditional branch files are generated: the investigation's bounded\n110\toutcomes (stable contract → build directly; schema in flux → build behind an\n111\tisolating adapter with contract tests; not yet consumable → defer Tier 2)\n112\tchange the approach or timing of the same epics, not which epics exist —\n113\tevery outcome still delivers the same report service, administrator view,\n114\tquota validation, and documentation. The gate is therefore recorded as\n115\t`gated_by` plus `gate_failure_impact` (action `rewrite`) on E002 rather than\n116\tas per-branch epic sets.\n117\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a15e16227a0f409a","agent_id":"ab1f8f295b5078e25","session_id":"d5dcf521-f769-4ae5-8dc7-46406c9edf08","uuid":"9a925bf7-c5af-4de7-b671-ab6b05c3221d","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:13:29.811Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b72e8ac6053b0e41","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
